<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Polish–Muscovite War (1605–1618)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1605">1605</a>–<a href="/years/1618">1618</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Dymitriads · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Polish–Muscovite War of 1605–1618 was a series of conflicts in which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> intervened in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/time-of-troubles">Time of Troubles</a>, first through the pretenders known as False Dmitry and from 1609 openly under Sigismund III. After the victory at Klushino (1610) Polish troops held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> until 1612, when a Russian national rising drove them out. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/truce-of-deulino">Truce of Deulino</a> (1618) left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a> and other western lands to the Commonwealth.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/polish-muscovite-war-1605-1618.jpg" alt="Polish–Muscovite War (1605–1618)" width="320" height="251" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rzeczpospolita_Dymitriads.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> had been in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/time-of-troubles">Time of Troubles</a> since the death of Tsar Feodor I in 1598, which ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rurik-dynasty">Rurik dynasty</a>, and a famine from 1601 to 1603 deepened the crisis. In late 1600 a Polish mission led by Chancellor Lew Sapieha proposed an alliance with a future personal union, but Tsar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boris-godunov">Boris Godunov</a> declined and instead extended the Treaty of Jam Zapolski to 1622. The impostor False Dmitry I appeared in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in 1603 and found backing among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a> such as Michał Wiśniowiecki and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sapieha-family">Sapieha family</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-iii-vasa">Sigismund III Vasa</a> gave him only 4,000 zlotys, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> also funded him. In Polish historiography these first interventions, the First Dymitriad (1604–1606) and Second Dymitriad (1607–1609), are a conflict of their own.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In 1607 False Dmitry II appeared, again supported by Polish magnates, who supplied about 7,500 soldiers. On 28 February 1609 Tsar Vasili Shuysky allied with Charles IX of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, and Sigismund, whose main aim was to regain the Swedish throne, obtained the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejm">Sejm</a>'s permission to declare war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. Boyars opposed to Shuysky asked for Prince Władysław as tsar, and in September 1610 the Seven Boyars elected him. Sigismund refused to let his son take the throne unless the Muscovites converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>, and the pro-Polish boyars withdrew their support.</p><p>A rising in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> against the Polish garrison in 1611 led to a siege of the Kremlin. Patriarch Hermogenes, who reportedly refused to order the attack called off, was starved to death. In 1611 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuzma-minin">Kuzma Minin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmitry-pozharsky">Prince Dmitry Pozharsky</a> raised a new army, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> were ousted from Moscow in September 1612, although they had taken <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a> in June 1611. The front stayed quiet until a new campaign began on 6 April 1617, nominally under Władysław and in fact under Hetman Chodkiewicz. Cossacks under Petro Sahaidachny joined in 1618, and the siege of Moscow, begun on 2 October, was lifted after a night assault on 10–11 October failed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>A Russian assembly elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-of-russia">Michael Romanov</a> tsar in 1613, beginning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-romanov">Romanov dynasty</a>, which ruled until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/february-revolution">February Revolution</a> of 1917. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/truce-of-deulino">Truce of Deulino</a>, concluded on 11 December 1618, gave the Commonwealth Chernigov, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/severia">Severia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a> and set a fifteen-year truce. Władysław kept his claim to the Russian throne, though Sigismund had given it up. The victory was very costly in money and lives. Fighting resumed in 1632 in the Smolensk War, and by the Treaty of Polyanovka of 1634 Władysław renounced his claim. The war was the first major sign of a rivalry between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> that lasted for centuries, and it inspired Pushkin's play <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boris-godunov">Boris Godunov</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Polish–Russian War (1609–1618)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
